WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.679
Coronavirus isn’t dangerous for me so I’m
not worried.

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:08.500
And anyway, the chance I’ll actually get
it are super small.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:15.430
These seem like 2 of the most common reasons
I hear for why someone isn’t doing much

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:16.430
about Covid19.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:22.099
I want to convince you both those reasons
make no sense and that staying home is an

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:28.869
effective thing to do so you really should
if you can.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:35.630
I finished filming this before the news that
the US government now recommends no more than

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.550
10 people to be in the same place.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.910
I think this is excellent news and I’m really
really relieved to hear it.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.120
I hope other countries follow suit.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.670
The rest of this video will explain why I
think you should follow this advice, whether

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.720
you’re in America or not.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:57.000
It is true that, if you’re young and healthy,
your own chances of death from this disease

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.200
are low.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.420
But let’s say you were to get sick.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.369
At the current rate you’re likely to spread
it to 2 or 3 people.

00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:09.600
Are the people you’re in regular contact
with all healthy too?

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.780
Perhaps they are, but remember, each of them
will spread it to 2 or 3 more people, and

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.270
they each spread it to 2 or 3 themselves and
so on.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:24.729
There are going to be vulnerable people fairly
close to you that that virus reaches directly

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:31.260
because you got sick; an elderly person, someone
with diabetes, someone who has chronic respiratory

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.260
problems.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:39.090
For me, this would be my grandma, my dad,
and my cousin and looking at those death probabilities

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.200
for people I care about makes it real for
me.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.020
And remember each of the people you might
spread the virus to has their own people to

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.009
be worrying about as well.

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:55.670
If you reduce the number of people you infect,
that has a huge positive ripple-on effect.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:01.850
You are directly responsible for the lives
of people, some you know, many who you don’t.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.469
But, maybe you don’t think you’re likely
to get the disease at all, so it doesn’t

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:06.469
matter.

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:09.899
At the moment in the USA the number of confirmed
cases is only a few thousand, compared to

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:13.230
a population of a few hundred million.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:18.819
So I agree, those odds are still very good,
but they’re changing super fast.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:22.560
Even a week ago, coronavirus didn’t seem
anywhere near as bad as it does now, right?

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.049
Honestly, I was surprised at that too, until
I stopped to do a little calculation.

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:32.470
You’ve probably heard the number of cases
being reported daily in the news.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.570
But those numbers aren’t so important.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.349
What’s important is the answer to this question:

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:45.970
How long does it take for the number of cases
in your country to go up by a factor of 10,

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.209
so to get 10 times bigger?

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:52.939
For example, there are roughly 5,000 cases
in the USA right now (March 17th).

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:56.640
How much longer until you have 50,000 cases?

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.720
A couple of months maybe?

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.080
A few weeks?

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.840
That’s not it.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:09.840
If things keep following the same trends in
the USA, the number of cases goes up by a

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.270
factor of 10 in around one week.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:19.310
So if nothing changes, next week it’s 50,000,
more than the number in Italy right now.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:25.799
In two weeks it would be 500,000, and in just
3 weeks it would 5 million.

00:03:25.799 --> 00:03:31.280
It’s a similar rate of growth in most other
countries that haven’t yet taken drastic

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.239
measures like lockdowns.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:38.090
If you remember no other statistic from this
video, just remember this one: if we don’t

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:45.560
take drastic measures, the problem gets 10
times worse in a week, every single week.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.879
If people are still allowed to go outside
freely for example like in these countries

00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:55.760
right now, and if this roughly the number
of cases there now, next week you will have

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.840
10 times that, and again the week after, and
again.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.540
But it doesn’t need to be this way.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:09.109
Our main focus needs to be on increasing this
time from a week to much longer so the spread

00:04:09.109 --> 00:04:10.439
is slower.

00:04:10.439 --> 00:04:16.780
For example, Italy was until it’s lockdown
at a similar rate, 10 times worse in 1 week,

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.709
but has now slowed that time to about 17 days.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:27.180
We have to accept that the disease likely
still spread widely, but we want it to be

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.520
as slow motion as we can manage.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:35.060
That might sound like a stupid idea, I mean
why drag out the inevitable?

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.600
Well, hopefully you’ve seen this brilliant
inforgraphic from doctor Siouxsie Wiles that

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.600
explains it.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:46.870
If the number of cases keeps rising this fast,
we will have a lot of cases all at once and

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:47.870
suddenly.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.510
Then there will be way more sick people then
hospitals can treat at once.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:56.460
For example, in the USA there’s only about
350 thousand spare beds at a time for coronavirus

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:04.780
patients- that’s not even close to enough
and hospitals will be totally swamped if things

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.780
keep going like this.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.949
Once that happens, doctors will have to start
choosing who should live because not everyone

00:05:10.949 --> 00:05:11.949
can be cared for.

00:05:11.949 --> 00:05:15.860
On the other hand, if we can seriously slow
the disease spread then there’s way less

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.900
people sick at the one time and we might just
have enough healthcare to go around.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.780
This isn’t hypothetical either.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:25.781
You might have heard that the death rate of
Covid 19 is less than 1%, but that’s in

00:05:25.781 --> 00:05:28.870
places where everyone can still get treatement.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:33.979
In the places that have been overwhelmed,
the death rate is more like 3-5%, but it might

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:38.530
become even much higher if a healthcare system
is truly overworked.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.800
This is why we need to slow it down, because
we don’t want to be in the position that

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:51.191
1 in 20 people who get sick die, not because
they had to, but because we didn’t have

00:05:51.191 --> 00:05:53.870
the resources to save them.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.889
So how can we slow the spread?

00:05:55.889 --> 00:06:00.350
Here’s a useful simulation from the Washington
Post that explains it.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.710
Here the blue dots represent healthy people,
but brown dots represent people with the virus.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:09.780
When an infected person comes into contact
with someone else, they will spread the virus

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:10.780
to them.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.370
When a person with the virus recovers, they
become purple to show that they can no longer

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:17.720
infect or be infected.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.310
Let’s see what happens when everyone is
allowed to mingle freely, like they still

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.949
are in a lot of places.

00:06:25.949 --> 00:06:32.340
As you can see, very very quickly, there’s
a huge number of people sick at the same time.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:36.190
This is when hospitals will be jammed and
doctors are working until they collapse or

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:37.930
get ill.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.590
How can we slow it down?

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:43.780
Well, what if we ask everyone to stay home
if they can?

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.800
Limit contact with others as much as possible?

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.729
Let’s simulate this by getting most of these
dots to stop moving to represent people staying

00:06:52.729 --> 00:06:54.310
at home.

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:59.409
There will still be some people who can’t,
for example because they can’t work from

00:06:59.409 --> 00:07:00.409
home.

00:07:00.409 --> 00:07:04.639
These people who are moving are still able
to spread the disease, but see how slow that

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:05.750
spread is now?

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:09.710
There’s way fewer people sick at any one
time.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:15.430
There’s 2 main reasons staying at home like
this works:

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:22.210
Your probability of getting sick is much lower,
since you’re not meeting infected people

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:28.080
as much
and if you do get sick, you’re not so likely

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.840
to see someone else and give it to them.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.660
This is social distancing and it saves lives.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:38.160
Companies can save lives by asking employees
to work from home if possible.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.000
Universities and schools can save lives by
having classes remotely or by simply shutting

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.160
down.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.169
Religious leaders can save lives by asking
their congregation to pray from home rather

00:07:49.169 --> 00:07:50.870
than gathering.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:55.340
Event organizers can save lives by cancelling
matches and festivals.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:08:00.120
And governments can save lives by making all
of this mandatory.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:05.340
And you can save lives too- by avoiding nonessential
gatherings, even things like going to dinner

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:06.610
with friends.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:12.759
Especially if you have the most common symptoms
like a fever or a dry cough, you will be likely

00:08:12.759 --> 00:08:15.960
to save lives by isolating yourself.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.330
But let me be clear, you should stay home
even if you aren’t sick.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.840
Over 20% of infections come from people without
symtoms yet.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.710
From people who think that they’re fine
and so continue to go about their lives meeting

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:34.120
others and inadvertantly making them sick.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:40.659
These are all the reasons social distancing
makes sense, but I know it’s tough.

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:44.229
You’re probably thinking you’ll go insane
if you have to spend so much time alone.

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:46.960
There are things you can do though to make
it easier.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:54.029
Firstly, call your family, friends and colleagues
for no reason but just to chat.

00:08:54.029 --> 00:08:57.930
For example, the place where I am going to
start a job soon is now completely working

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:04.360
from home- but they have video calls at lunch
so everyone can still eat together virtually.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:11.730
It’s important to talk to someone at least
once a day, so call your friends.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.240
Secondly, look after your physical health
too.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:27.850
You only need to avoid people, not daylight
so you can still go out for walks.

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:34.430
Lastly, try keep yourself productively occupied
in your spare time so it doesn’t feel wasted.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.839
There’s only so many movies you can watch
before you get frustrated that you’re not

00:09:38.839 --> 00:09:40.890
doing anything.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:46.110
Take this as your chance to learn that thing
you want to learn, or to read more, or to

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:47.110
finish one of your projects.

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:48.110
I’m going to link a bunch of online resources
that I personally like that you can do for

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:49.110
free in your extra hours.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:53.290
The point is this though: staying inside will
save lives, so thank you for looking out for

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.650
others by doing this.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:59.160
But look after yourself too- keep yourself
from going crazy, Good luck!

